 {"q": "What 'crack' does Tolstoy find in his own reasoning at the end of chapter VII?", "options": ["That science had disproved God", "That reason, which says life is senseless, is itself the fruit of life, so its rejection of life seems self-defeating", "That his family needed him financially", "That Schopenhauer had changed his mind"], "answer": 1, "why": "'Reason is life's son... yet reason rejects life itself! I felt that there was something wrong here.'"},
 {"q": "Which best states Schellenberg's argument from divine hiddenness?", "options": ["God is hidden because he does not care", "A perfectly loving God would make relationship with himself possible for anyone not resisting; non-resisting unbelievers exist; so there is no such God", "God is hidden because people are sinful, so he must exist", "Hiddenness proves God is not all-powerful"], "answer": 1, "why": "The argument turns on non-resistant unbelief, people who want to believe and cannot."},
